  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nAfter what has been one of the driest and warmest fall seasons\n(Sep-Nov) on record in our forecast area, there are strong\nindications that a wetter and cooler weather pattern will take\nshape. There are three aspects of the forecast getting most\nattention including a light rainfall event tonight ahead of a cold\nfrontal passage, a more significant rainfall event for mid week\nassociated with a strong digging trough and cyclogenesis, and much\ncooler temperatures associated with a polar stream trough high\nsurface pressures over the north central to northeast CONUS. \n\nGoing through tonight into early Monday, showers and a few storms\nhave developed over Arkansas ahead of a cold front that is pushing\nsoutheast toward the area. There is sufficient instability for\nstorms, and wouldn't rule out some strong wind gusts and small hail\ngiven relatively steep lapse rates and strong deep layer shear as\nstorms move into northwest portions of our area. A few brief \ndownpours could bring localized amounts greater than half an inch,\nbut overall amounts should be around/less than a quarter of an \ninch on average as this activity continues to track across the \narea. By the time it reaches roughly the Natchez Trace Corridor, \nthere should be a weakening trend with only very light if any \nrainfall forecast.\n\nFrom Mon night into Tuesday, we'll see a temporary end to rain\nchances with cooler high pressure in control, but guidance overall\nhas trended stronger and more amplified with a digging trough the\nthe south central CONUS, and this brings confidence that we'll\nhave at least a 6 to 12 hr window of strong mid level ascent in\nthe late Tue to Wed time frame that supports widespread rainfall,\nespecially east of the MS River, where much guidance now shows \npotential for greater than half of an inch rainfall. Given the \ndrought conditions this is a sensitive forecast and will follow \nconservative WPC QPF for now, but there is some cautious optimism \nfor higher totals, especially in our eastern areas where deep\nconvection could at least locally enhance totals. So stay tuned \nto the latest forecasts. Probabilistic QPF forecasts that run \nthrough Tue night indicate a low (1 in 10 chance) prob for \ngreater than an inch, with rain continuing beyond that into Wed. \nOnce again, given the on-going drought, will keep explicit \nforecasts conservative for now and monitor trends.\n\nOtherwise, another forecast aspect will be the much cooler\ntemperatures that accompany the mid week rainfall. We should see\ntemperatures dip to their lowest values later this week in the\ndrier air following the mid week system, with the potential for \nfreezes both Thanksgiving and Friday mornings. /EC/",
